On a stretch of shingle beach near the English coast stand three strange concrete structures: a curved wall more than sixty meters long and two giant dishes {{1|facing, the sea}}. Built in the 1920s and 1930s, they {{2|was}} part of an experiment in hearing. Before radar existed, military planners hoped {{3|that sound could warn them of approaching aircraft}} before the planes came into view.

The idea was simple. A curved concrete surface gathers sound waves and concentrates them at a single point, {{4|where, a listener}} with a microphone could detect the drone of an engine miles away. In tests, the largest mirror could pick up aircraft roughly twenty miles off when conditions were ideal.

{{5|However,}} conditions were rarely ideal. Wind, waves, and the noise of nearby boats {{6|was drowning out}} faint engines, and as aircraft grew faster, the warning the mirrors provided shrank to just a few minutes. {{7|By the late 1930s, radar, which could detect planes far more reliably and at much greater distances, had made the mirrors obsolete.}}

Today the mirrors are protected {{8|landmarks, visitors}} can tour them on guided walks a few times a year. Standing in front of the largest one, a visitor who speaks quietly can be heard clearly by {{9|whomever}} stands at the focal point dozens of meters away. {{10|The site also has a small parking lot near the beach.}}

When the town of Alder Falls replaced its old streetlights in 2015, few residents {{1|expected, that}} the change would make news. The new fixtures, {{2|which pointed}} their light downward instead of outward, were chosen mainly to save electricity. Within a year, however, the town had become a destination for stargazers.

{{3|The town also hosts a popular summer fair each July.}} Under the old lights, which scattered their glow in every direction, only the brightest stars had been visible. Now, on clear nights, visitors spread blankets across the town park and watched meteors until dawn. The local diner began staying open past midnight, and two motels that had nearly closed were suddenly full on {{4|weekends; their}} owners credited the darkness.

Not everyone was pleased at first. A group of shop owners {{5|were}} worried that customers would feel unsafe on darker sidewalks. {{6|For example,}} the town council studied police records and found that reported crimes had not increased since the change. The council voted {{7|unanimously}} to keep the new lights and later passed a rule requiring that any new outdoor lights in town be shielded {{8|so that their light falls only downward toward the ground below them.}}

Today, Alder Falls holds a star party every August. Astronomers set up telescopes on the {{9|librarys'}} lawn, and children line up to see the rings of Saturn. {{10|The darkness that once worried shop owners is now part of the town's routine.}}

In April 1956, a converted oil tanker called the Ideal-X left Newark, New Jersey, bound for Houston, Texas. Its cargo was unremarkable: fifty-eight metal boxes {{1|that were full of ordinary, everyday, common goods}}. What made the voyage historic was the boxes themselves.

Before the 1950s, ships were loaded {{2|piece by piece. Dockworkers}} carried sacks, crates, and barrels aboard by hand or with small cranes, fitting each item into the hold like a piece of a puzzle. A ship could sit in port for a week or more while {{3|it's}} cargo was loaded and unloaded, and goods were often damaged or stolen along the way.

Malcom McLean, the trucking businessman {{4|whom organized}} the Ideal-X voyage, had spent years watching his trucks wait at crowded docks. His idea was simple: instead of unloading a truck's contents {{5|onto the dock. Why not lift}} the entire trailer body onto the ship? Containers of standard sizes could then move from truck to ship to train without ever being opened.

[1] The idea did not catch on overnight. [2] Ports had to build enormous new {{6|cranes and shipping}} companies had to agree on standard container sizes. [3] Dockworkers, many of whom feared for their jobs, resisted the change. [4] Within a few decades, however, containers had become the backbone of world trade. {{7|}}

Today, a single large container ship can carry the equivalent of more than twenty thousand twenty-foot containers, {{8|it}} can be unloaded in a few days. Shipping now accounts for only a small fraction of the price of most products. {{9|However,}} the toy, the shirt, or the phone on a store shelf may well have crossed an ocean in a box much like the ones aboard the Ideal-X. {{10|}}

[1] In 1950, several members of a Minneapolis chapter of Hadassah, a Jewish women's organization, brought a request to a local engineer named H. David Dalquist. [2] Some of them remembered a heavy, ring-shaped cake pan from their families' kitchens in Europe, {{1|and they wanted}} a lighter version that could be bought in American stores.

[3] {{2|Dalquist, whose small company,}} Nordic Ware, made kitchen products from cast aluminum, agreed to try. [4] The pan he designed had fluted sides and a tube in the center {{3|that let heat reach}} the middle of a dense batter. [5] He gave the pan a name, "Bundt," that his company could register as a trademark. {{5|}}

[6] For more than a decade, the pan sold {{4|poor}}. [7] {{6|One reason was that many}} shoppers had never heard of the ring-shaped cake it was meant to bake.

[8] That changed in 1966, when a home baker from Texas named Ella Helfrich entered a recipe in the Pillsbury Bake-Off, a national cooking contest. [9] {{7|Baked in a Bundt pan, a ring of soft fudge formed inside her "Tunnel of Fudge" cake.}} [10] The cake won second place, but it soon became {{8|the contests}} best-known entry. [11] The flood of requests from bakers who wanted to try the recipe {{9|were}} so large that Nordic Ware had to expand production to keep up.

[12] Today, the pan that Dalquist designed for a small group of club members is a fixture in kitchens across the country. [13] Its name, once known only in a few Minneapolis homes, now appears in cookbooks everywhere. {{10|}}

[1] After serving in the U.S. Navy during the Civil War, Lewis Latimer was hired at a Boston patent law firm as an office {{1|boy, his duties}} were mostly running errands. [2] Watching the firm's draftsmen prepare drawings for patent applications, {{2|Latimer taught himself the craft}} at night. [3] His skill eventually earned him a promotion to head draftsman. [4] In 1876, he prepared the drawings {{3|that}} accompanied Alexander Graham Bell's application for a telephone patent.

[5] Latimer then turned to electric lighting. [6] Early incandescent bulbs used carbon filaments that burned out quickly, and manufacturing them {{4|were}} slow and unreliable. [7] In 1882, Latimer patented an improved process for making carbon {{5|filaments, the process}} yielded filaments that were cheaper to produce. [8] {{6|However,}} he supervised the installation of electric lighting systems in several cities, including New York and Philadelphia.

[9] In 1884, Latimer joined Thomas Edison's company, where he {{7|worked and was employed}} as a draftsman and as an expert witness in patent cases. [10] He was later the only Black member of the Edison Pioneers, a group of {{8|Edisons}} earliest associates. [11] Latimer, who also wrote poetry and taught drafting to immigrants, {{9|died in 1928.}}

[1] In 1965, the chemist Stephanie Kwolek was searching for a lightweight fiber strong enough to replace the steel wires in car tires. [2] One of the polymer solutions she prepared looked {{1|wrong. It}} was thin and cloudy, while successful solutions were usually thick and clear. [3] Most chemists would have thrown it out.

[4] Kwolek, {{2|who's}} curiosity outweighed her doubts, asked a technician to spin the solution into fibers. [5] He refused at first, worried that the cloudy liquid would clog the spinning machine. [6] She persuaded him to try, and the fibers that came out were remarkably {{3|strong and stiff, and they weighed little}}. [7] {{4|Tested in the laboratory, the fibers proved}} several times stronger than steel of the same weight.

[8] DuPont, the company where Kwolek worked, developed the material into a product called Kevlar, which went on sale in the early 1970s. [9] {{5|However,}} it is used in bicycle tires, firefighters' gear, and the protective vests worn by police officers. {{6|}}

[10] Kwolek later said that she had never set out to invent anything {{7|dramatic, she}} simply paid attention to a result that did not fit. [11] She received many honors for her work, {{8|such as being}} induction into the National Inventors Hall of Fame. [12] {{9|The vests made from her fiber have since protected thousands of officers.}}

[1] In 1957, two engineers in New Jersey, Alfred Fielding and Marc Chavannes, set out to make a new kind of wallpaper. [2] They sealed two plastic sheets together in a way that trapped rows of air {{1|bubbles, they hoped}} the textured surface would appeal to home decorators.

[3] Almost no one wanted it. [4] The engineers then tried to sell the material as insulation for {{2|greenhouses, which}} also failed. [5] Neither of these {{3|attempts were}} successful, but the inventors noticed something else about their product: it cushioned whatever it was wrapped around.

[6] {{4|As a result,}} in 1960 they founded a company, Sealed Air, to sell the material for packaging. [7] One of their earliest customers was IBM, which used the bubbly sheets to protect {{5|it's}} new computers during shipping. {{6|}}

[8] Today the material is sold in rolls in nearly every office-supply store, and many people find that {{7|popping}} the bubbles is oddly satisfying. [9] Some fans even celebrate an annual Bubble Wrap Appreciation Day {{8|on, the last Monday in January}}. [10] {{9|The product that failed as wallpaper had found its true purpose.}}

[1] In 1887, {{1|a young clerk in a Washington, D.C., hat store named Matthew Henson}} was hired by Robert Peary, a naval engineer who needed an assistant for a survey in Nicaragua. [2] Peary soon recognized that Henson was far more than an assistant. [3] Over the next two decades, Henson {{2|accompanies}} Peary on seven expeditions to the Arctic.

[4] Unlike most members of the expeditions, Henson learned the Inuit language, and he became expert at {{3|driving dog sleds, building igloos, and he hunted}} for food. [5] On April 6, 1909, Peary, Henson, and four Inuit men reached what they believed was the North Pole. {{4|}}

[6] When the expedition returned, Peary received {{5|most of the credit; Henson}} was largely ignored. [7] Recognition {{6|were}} slow to come. [8] In 1944, Congress awarded Henson a medal, and in 1988 his remains {{7|was moved}} to Arlington National Cemetery, where he now lies {{8|alongside and next to}} Peary. [9] {{9|In death, at least, the two explorers were finally side by side.}}

[1] In 1858, Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux won a competition to design a large public park in the middle of {{1|Manhattan, their}} plan, which they called the Greensward Plan, imagined meadows, woods, and lakes where swamps and rocky outcrops then stood.

[2] Building the park took years of labor. [3] Workers blasted rock, moved earth, and planted {{2|many thousands of trees}}. [4] {{3|Although}} the finished park looks as if nature had made it, almost every hill and pond was designed. [5] The designers {{4|also tucked roads for crosstown traffic}} below the level of the park, so that carriages could cross it without disturbing visitors. {{5|}}

[6] The park's creation also had a cost that was long overlooked. [7] To clear the land, the city used its power of eminent domain to take property from the people who lived there, including the roughly 225 residents of Seneca Village, a community that was about two-thirds African American and in which more than half of the Black {{6|household's}} owned their land. [8] The village's {{7|churches, homes, and school, were}} demolished.

[9] Today, Central Park {{8|draws}} millions of visitors each year. [10] {{9|Recent historians have worked to make sure that its full history, including Seneca Village, is remembered.}}
